-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5881a7d5-59e2-52c6-94a7-1ea9049b07d8', 'rg10-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading Between the Lines of a Listing$q$, $q$Anyone shopping for a used bicycle online soon discovers that sellers speak a language of their own. A careful buyer learns to translate it before handing over any money.

Take the phrase "gently used." In a perfect world, it would mean a bike that was ridden to the library a few times and then parked in a dry garage. In practice, it can describe almost anything that still has two wheels. The phrase is a euphemism, a soft expression chosen to make a plain fact sound more pleasant.

Other phrases follow the same pattern. A bike with "character" may have scratched paint and a squeaky chain. A frame that "needs a little love" might need new brakes, new tires and an afternoon of work. A listing that says "priced to sell" often means the seller simply wants it gone, which can be good news for a buyer who knows how to make repairs.

None of this language is exactly dishonest. Sellers are not inventing problems or hiding them completely; they are choosing the most flattering words available. The trouble comes when buyers read those words literally and skip the inspection.

A smart shopper therefore asks direct questions. How old are the tires? Do the gears shift smoothly? Has the frame ever been bent? Better still, the shopper meets the seller in a public place during daylight, squeezes the brakes, spins each wheel and takes a short ride.

Language that sounds cheerful is not the enemy. It is simply a clue. When a listing works hard to sound charming, a buyer should work just as hard to look closely.$q$, 'draft')
