-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('621ead57-d96b-532a-8dc5-8ef6ef0fc115', 'rg10-figurative-language-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cue Sheet$q$, $q$Tech rehearsal began at four o'clock, and by six the auditorium had become what the stage manager, Ifeoma Adeyemi, cheerfully called organized chaos. Actors in half-finished costumes wandered the aisles. A ladder stood in the middle of the stage. Someone in the booth kept testing a thunder sound effect, and each time it rolled through the room, the cast jumped.

Ifeoma sat at a folding table with a headset, a flashlight and a cue sheet covered in pencil marks. Her job was to call every lighting and sound change in the play, more than ninety of them. When she spoke into the headset, the crew moved. When she was silent, they waited.

At seven, the lighting board froze. The stage went dark in the middle of a scene, and the lead actor, Caleb Morrow, stood blinking in the single beam of an exit sign. In the booth, the operator, Hana Sato, tried every button she could think of.

"We are experiencing a brief intermission," Ifeoma announced to the cast, as calmly as a pilot reporting light turbulence. A few people laughed, and the tension in the room loosened.

It took twenty minutes to restart the board. When the lights finally rose again, the whole cast applauded Hana as if she had just performed a solo.

The rehearsal ended at nine, well past schedule. The director gathered everyone onstage and said, with a tired smile, that it had been "a very educational evening." Ifeoma wrote that phrase at the top of her cue sheet. Three days later, on opening night, every one of her ninety cues landed exactly on time, and she kept the sheet as a reminder of how they got there.$q$, 'draft')
