values
  ('f51f856e-e423-5154-8bb3-32022ea551f9', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'easy',
   $q$The author says the name "living stones" sounds like "a contradiction." Why?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Because stones are not alive, so pairing \"living\" with \"stones\" seems impossible.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Because scientists disagree about where the plants were first found.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"Because the plants die quickly whenever they are moved to a new place.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Because the plants are actually stones that were mistaken for plants.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because stones are not alive, so pairing "living" with "stones" seems impossible.$q$, $q$The oxymoron joins opposites, something alive and something lifeless, yet it fits plants that "look almost exactly like pebbles." The passage is clear that they are real plants with leaves and flowers, not stones.$q$, 'draft', 'e7e59545-576f-5455-8b37-f3bcf51196e8')
