-- Passage rg10-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('800edc91-6bef-5e00-aa9d-08739653e7d0', 'rg10-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Houses That Came by Rail$q$, $q$In the early 1900s, a family in the fictional prairie county of Vellin could choose a house the way they chose a coat: from a catalog. Several lumber companies printed thick books of house designs, each with a drawing, a floor plan and a price. A buyer mailed in an order, and weeks later a railroad car arrived holding nearly everything needed to build the home, from numbered boards to doorknobs and window glass.

These kit houses solved a real problem. Many small towns in Vellin had no architect and few skilled carpenters. Lumber was expensive to ship in small amounts, and buying each piece separately from different suppliers took time. A kit bundled the design, the materials and the instructions into a single purchase. Because each board was cut and numbered at the factory, a family working with one experienced builder could often raise the frame in a few weeks.

The catalogs also shaped how towns looked. Since thousands of buyers chose from the same few dozen designs, the same porch or roofline appeared in towns hundreds of miles apart. Local historian Teodor Vasquez-Lund has identified more than three hundred surviving kit houses in Vellin County alone, many of which were once thought to be ordinary homes built by local hands. Vasquez-Lund matches them to catalog plans by measuring rooms and checking for faint numbers stamped on the beams.

The kits were not perfect. Buyers sometimes received missing or damaged pieces, and some designs suited a mild climate better than the prairie winters. Over time, as more towns gained lumberyards and trained builders, the business faded, and most companies stopped selling kits by the 1940s.

Yet the houses remain. For many Vellin families, a kit was the first home they owned, and the numbered beams in their attics still show how ordinary people used the railroad and the mail to build communities quickly.$q$, 'draft')
