   $q$How does the example of the Linden Hall wall hanging develop the central idea?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It explains how wall hangings were woven in the region two hundred years ago.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It shows the rule in practice: the treatment supports the hanging, leaves honest gaps and can be undone later.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that visitors were pleased the hanging had been made to look new again.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-detail"},{"text":"It shows that the rule is rarely followed, because most conservators fill holes with new weaving.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows the rule in practice: the treatment supports the hanging, leaves honest gaps and can be undone later.$q$, $q$Reyes-Whitcombe stitched the hanging to linen instead of reweaving it, and "the stitches can be snipped," which matches both parts of the rule. Visitors asked why gaps remained, so the hanging was not made to look new.$q$, 'draft', 'f0f47798-5f61-5692-b34e-a0cf519c10fe')
