  ('57b43195-d5eb-53cd-a000-370ef21c7188', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-main-idea-summary'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Which is the best objective summary of the passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Old repairs used glue and new thread. These repairs yellowed, stiffened and pulled against the old threads, and they made it hard to tell which parts of objects were original.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"Conservators are the most patient and talented workers in any museum. Visitors who complain about gaps in old hangings simply do not understand art and should trust the experts.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"includes-opinion"},{"text":"Textile conservators now do as little as possible and keep treatments reversible, a rule learned from past repairs that caused harm. At Linden Hall, a hanging was stitched to linen, leaving honest gaps, an approach that takes great skill.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Museums used to make tapestries look new, but they now leave them damaged. Conservators no longer repair holes at all, because doing nothing is always the safest choice.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"distorts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Textile conservators now do as little as possible and keep treatments reversible, a rule learned from past repairs that caused harm. At Linden Hall, a hanging was stitched to linen, leaving honest gaps, an approach that takes great skill.$q$, $q$The summary beginning "Textile conservators now do as little as possible" covers the rule, its origin, the example and the conclusion neutrally. The summary claiming conservators "no longer repair holes at all" distorts the passage, which says doing less only "can seem like doing nothing" and still involves stitching and support.$q$, 'draft', 'f0f47798-5f61-5692-b34e-a0cf519c10fe')
