-- Passage rg10-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4d293e7f-c0a0-577f-a6c7-324e19dd44ae', 'rg10-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Second Pair of Gloves$q$, $q$Ines Okafor had worked at the Brightwater glass studio every Saturday since September, but Mr. Vasquez still had not let her touch the furnace. Her job was to sweep, to label the finished bowls, and to watch. She told her friends she was learning a lot, and she said it the way people say they love a gift they plan to return.

This Saturday a large order of blue vases had to be finished by noon. Mr. Vasquez worked at the bench, rolling the long pipe across the steel rails, while his assistant, Dov, opened the furnace door for each new gather of glass. At ten o'clock Dov's phone buzzed. He read the message, turned pale, and said his sister had gone to the hospital to have her baby. Mr. Vasquez told him to go, and he went without taking off his apron.

For a while Mr. Vasquez worked alone, opening the furnace door with his foot and losing the heat from each piece before he could shape it. Two vases cracked in the cooling oven. He set the third pipe down and looked across the room at Ines, who was pretending to sort labels.

"You have watched Dov a hundred times," he said. "Tell me what he does with the door."

"He opens it only when your pipe is ready, and he stands to the side so the heat doesn't hit his face."

He handed her Dov's heavy gloves. They were too big, and she had to grip the handle with both hands. Her first opening was late, and the glass came out lumpy. Her second was right on time. By eleven thirty they had found a rhythm, and neither of them spoke except to count.

At noon the last vase went into the oven. Mr. Vasquez wiped his face, looked at the gloves on Ines's hands, and said nothing about them. The next Saturday, there was a second pair hanging on the hook beside Dov's, smaller, with her initials written on the cuff in white marker.$q$, 'draft')
