-- Passage rg10-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('159dae90-fd07-5b66-9a26-5393df1ab587', 'rg10-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Weather Ledger$q$, $q$Priya Lindqvist had volunteered at the county historical society for three weeks before anyone gave her something more interesting than envelopes. Then Ms. Achebe, the archivist, set a gray box on the reading table and asked her to scan a weather ledger kept by a farmer named Tobias Wren between 1891 and 1903.

The ledger was mostly numbers: morning temperature, evening temperature, inches of rain, the direction of the wind. Tobias had used the same blue-black ink and the same narrow columns for twelve years. Priya scanned forty pages before she noticed that the entries for July 1897 were written in pencil, in a rounder hand, with the temperatures recorded to the half degree instead of the whole.

"Different person," Ms. Achebe said when Priya showed her. She did not seem surprised. She opened a folder of family letters and found one from that August, written by Tobias's daughter, Hester, to her aunt. "Father has been laid up with his leg all summer," Hester wrote, "so I have taken over the readings, and I confess I am more particular about them than he is."

Priya went back to the ledger. The pencil entries continued through September and stopped. In October the blue-black ink returned, but now the temperatures were recorded to the half degree, and they stayed that way until the last page in 1903.

"So he went back to doing it himself," Priya said.

"He did," Ms. Achebe said. "Look more carefully at how."

Priya looked. Beside the first ink entry in October, squeezed into the margin in small letters, Tobias had written two words: Hester's method.

Ms. Achebe asked her to add a note to the catalog record so that future researchers would know who had kept which pages. Priya wrote the note, read it over, and then added a second sentence explaining why the data became more precise after 1897. It took her twenty minutes to get the wording right, which was longer than she had spent on anything at the historical society so far.$q$, 'draft')
