-- Passage rg10-story-details-inference-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c1a1a6bd-05b3-55c3-beb0-33d8d34d3ed7', 'rg10-story-details-inference-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Weathervane and the Kite$q$, $q$On the roof of a barn stood an iron weathervane shaped like a rooster, and every afternoon a paper kite flew over the field below. The kite was red, with a tail of knotted ribbons, and it climbed and dipped and turned somersaults in the air while the children holding its string shouted with joy.

"You poor thing," the kite called down one day. "You have stood on that roof for forty years and never once left it. You only spin in place. Don't you wish you could fly as I do?"

The weathervane creaked as it turned. "I go where the wind tells me," it said, "and so do you."

The kite laughed. "I go wherever I like. Watch." And it swooped low over the barn and then shot upward until it was nearly lost against the clouds.

That evening the wind shifted. It came hard from the north, and the farmer, stepping out to look at the sky, saw the iron rooster swing round to face the hills. He called to his sons to bring the hay wagon in before the storm, and they had it under the roof just as the first rain fell.

In the field, the children had run for home, and the kite had been left tangled in the branches of an oak with its string snapped. Its paper was soaked through, and its ribbons hung limp.

The next morning the farmer's youngest son climbed the oak, freed the kite, and carried it to the barn to dry. He laid it on a beam near the roof, close enough to hear the weathervane creak above.

"I flew higher than you ever will," the kite said weakly.

"You did," said the weathervane. "And yet it was my turning that brought the wagon in."$q$, 'draft')
