-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ff721d1f-deff-5131-a84a-aa5adb4a4a8b', 'rg10-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Canal That Came Too Soon$q$, $q$In 1838 the merchants of Halvorsen Crossing finished a canal linking their town to the Tamsin River, twenty-two miles to the east. They had spent eleven years and nearly all of the town's savings on the project. Four years later, a railroad line arrived, and within a decade the canal carried almost no freight. For more than a century, local histories described the canal as a costly mistake, a monument to bad timing.

That verdict is understandable. Barges moved at the pace of the mules that pulled them, about three miles an hour, while trains crossed the same distance in less than an hour. Shippers who could choose the railroad did so, and the canal company went bankrupt in 1851.

Yet records recently examined by historian Teodora Mbeki suggest a different reading. The railroad did not choose Halvorsen Crossing by chance. Its planners chose the town precisely because the canal had already turned it into a busy market, with warehouses, a bank and a population that had tripled since 1830. In letters to investors, the railroad's chief surveyor named the canal traffic as the main reason the town deserved a station.

Seen this way, the canal did not fail so much as hand its work to a faster successor. It built the commerce that the railroad then captured. The town's later prosperity, which lasted well into the next century, rested on a foundation the canal had laid.

Today, a walking path follows the old towpath, and a small sign calls the canal "a bold idea overtaken by progress." Mbeki's research suggests the sign should be rewritten. The canal was not simply overtaken by progress. It helped make that progress possible.$q$, 'draft')
