-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0ae9bdc9-dbc0-578e-bc09-59cfaf026bba', 'rg10-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How the Kessler Footbridge Listens$q$, $q$The Kessler Footbridge, which carries about nine thousand walkers and cyclists across the Orrin River each day, looks like an ordinary steel span. Hidden along its underside, though, are sixty small sensors that report, every second, how much the bridge stretches, bends and shakes. Engineers call the system a structural health monitor, and it works in three stages.

The first stage is measurement. Each sensor is a strain gauge: a thin strip of metal foil glued to a steel beam. When the beam stretches even slightly under a load, the foil stretches with it, and its electrical resistance changes by a tiny but measurable amount. Other sensors, called accelerometers, record how quickly the deck moves up and down.

The second stage is comparison. Readings travel by cable to a computer in a locked cabinet at the east end of the bridge. The computer compares each new reading with a record of how the bridge behaved during its first year, when it was known to be sound. A crowd on a festival day will produce large numbers, but numbers that match the pattern of earlier crowds are not a concern.

The third stage is alerting, and it is where the system earns its value. If a beam begins to stretch more than its history predicts under the same load, the computer sends a message to the city's bridge inspector, Marisol Achterberg. Last March, such a message led her team to a loose bolt plate near the center span, a problem that would have gone unnoticed until the next scheduled inspection, seven months later.

Sensors do not replace human inspectors, Achterberg points out. They tell inspectors where to look. On a bridge this busy, knowing where to look can make all the difference.$q$, 'draft')
