-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f16ea656-8663-5817-a00b-0e31a8febf9f', 'rg10-text-structure-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving the Halden Curtain$q$, $q$When the Halden Opera House reopened after a long renovation, its most famous feature was missing. The painted stage curtain, a 1912 scene of a harbor at dawn, had been rolled up in a storage room for thirty years. Unrolled at last, it was faded, water-stained and torn in more than forty places. The theater's board faced a question that every conservator knows well: should the curtain be restored to look new, or preserved to show its age?

The board first hired a commercial painting firm, which proposed repainting the whole scene in fresh, bright colors. The estimate was modest and the result would have been dramatic. But textile conservator Yuki Ferreira, invited to review the plan, warned that new paint would bury the original brushwork under a modern layer. She added that the stiff paint could crack the old fabric as the curtain was raised and lowered.

Ferreira proposed a slower method. Her team cleaned the surface with soft sponges and distilled water, then backed each tear with fine dyed netting stitched by hand. Where paint had flaked away entirely, they filled the gaps with watercolor in a slightly lighter shade than the original, so that a close viewer could tell the new work from the old. The process took fourteen months instead of three.

The finished curtain does not look new. The harbor is softer and paler than it was in 1912, and faint lines still mark where the worst tears were. Some board members were disappointed at first. Over time, though, many changed their minds, because audiences responded to the curtain as a survivor, an object with a history they could see.

The Halden curtain now rises every night before the performance. It is not the curtain the painter made, and it does not pretend to be. That honesty, Ferreira's team believes, is what makes it worth keeping.$q$, 'draft')
