-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d07486b2-1b40-5714-a741-dc8f1d79b972', 'rg10-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Turn Down the Lights in Corvel$q$, $q$On a clear night thirty years ago, a person standing in the town square of Corvel could see the band of the Milky Way stretching overhead. Today, from the same spot, a careful observer can count perhaps two dozen stars. Nothing has happened to the sky. What has changed is the ground: Corvel now lights its streets, parking lots and storefronts so brightly that the night has nearly disappeared.

Some residents will say that bright lights keep people safe, and safety is a reasonable concern. But much of Corvel's lighting does not light anything useful. Globe-shaped streetlamps throw as much light upward and sideways as they do onto the sidewalk. Parking lots at closed stores stay lit until dawn. A survey by the county planning office found that roughly a third of the town's outdoor light shines directly into the sky, where it helps no one walk home.

Wasted light also has costs that are easy to overlook. It adds to the town's electric bill, which taxpayers cover. It confuses migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate. And it disturbs the sleep of residents whose bedrooms face a glowing sign.

The fix does not require darkness. Shielded fixtures that point light downward can keep sidewalks just as bright while sending far less light upward. Timers can dim lots after businesses close. The neighboring town of Ashgrove made both changes four years ago. Its streets are no less safe, according to its police records, and its lighting costs dropped by a quarter.

Corvel's council should adopt a similar ordinance this year. The stars over the town square are not gone. They are only hidden, and we are the ones hiding them.$q$, 'draft')
