-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3b755bad-704b-5094-99a3-52fc89f615be', 'rg10-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Long Flight of the Amber Darter$q$, $q$Biologists long assumed that the amber darter, a small orange dragonfly common across the Velmar Plains, lived and died within a few miles of the pond where it hatched. The insect weighs less than a paper clip, and its wings seem too delicate for long journeys. Yet each autumn, observers along the southern coast reported swarms of amber darters appearing where none had bred.

To solve the puzzle, a team led by entomologist Kwame Lindqvist attached radio tags, each lighter than a grain of rice, to two hundred darters in the northern plains. Volunteers with handheld receivers followed the signals south. Several tagged insects traveled more than 300 miles in under two weeks, riding warm winds and resting in coastal marshes.

The finding answered one question but raised another. No tagged darter ever flew back north. If individuals did not return, how did the northern ponds fill with darters again each spring?

The answer came from a second study, which compared chemical traces in the wings of spring darters with the water of different regions. The spring arrivals in the north had grown up in southern marshes. In other words, the migration is a relay: one generation flies south in autumn, its offspring hatch in the south, and that new generation makes the return trip in spring.

The amber darter, then, is not a homebody after all, but neither is it a traveler in the way a single bird is. No individual completes the round trip. The journey belongs to the species, carried forward across generations, and Lindqvist's team now hopes to learn how each new generation knows which way to go.$q$, 'draft')
