-- Passage rg10-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('18c4b904-d097-5103-ada1-f885ef24e310', 'rg10-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Rain Gauges on the Back Fence$q$, $q$Every morning at seven, Leilani Osei walks to the fence behind her house in the hill town of Bresca, reads a clear plastic tube mounted on a post, and types a number into her phone. She is one of about four hundred volunteers in the Bresca Valley Rain Network, which has measured rainfall in backyards across the region for nineteen years. At first, many scientists doubted that such amateur data could be useful.

Their doubts were reasonable. Volunteers sometimes forget to read their gauges, place them under trees, or round their numbers. The national weather service already operated a dozen official stations in the valley, each with calibrated instruments and trained staff.

But a dozen stations could not capture how unevenly rain falls in hilly country. During a storm in 2019, the official station at the Bresca airport recorded less than an inch of rain. Volunteer gauges on the western slopes, only six miles away, recorded more than four inches, and the creeks below them flooded within hours. Forecasters who had relied on the airport number alone had underestimated the danger.

After that storm, the weather service began folding network data into its flood warnings. To address the early concerns, the network added training videos, standard gauge designs and an automatic check that flags any reading wildly different from its neighbors' readings. The volunteers' numbers are not as precise as an official station's, but there are thirty times as many of them, and in hill country, coverage often matters more than precision.

Osei's gauge collected a modest half inch this morning. Her number, alone, means little. Joined with hundreds of others, it becomes a map.$q$, 'draft')
