-- Passage rg10-theme-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('22ab2113-ca53-5255-9921-de940cc475f3', 'rg10-theme-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Kite and the String$q$, $q$High above a hillside meadow, a red kite rode the afternoon wind and complained to anyone who would listen.

"Look at the clouds," it said to a passing sparrow. "They drift wherever they like. Nothing pulls at them. But I have this string tied to my spine, and every time I try to climb higher, it drags me back toward the girl on the hill."

The sparrow tilted its head. "Have you ever flown without it?"

"Never," said the kite. "That is exactly my point."

All afternoon the kite tugged and twisted. It snapped against the line in every gust, until at last the string near the girl's spool wore thin. With one hard jerk, it parted.

For a single glorious moment the kite shot upward. Then the wind that had held it so steadily seemed to pass straight through it. The kite flipped once, twice, and began to fall in long, helpless spirals. It landed in the top branches of an oak at the edge of the meadow, where it hung with its tail knotted around a twig.

The sparrow landed beside it. "You are certainly free now," it said, not unkindly.

The kite did not answer.

Near sunset the girl climbed the oak. She worked the kite loose, smoothed its torn corner, and tied the broken string together with a careful double knot. The next morning she carried it back up the hill.

When the wind caught it this time, the kite climbed more slowly. It felt the string tighten as it rose, and for the first time it noticed that the pull was what kept its face turned into the wind. The string no longer felt like a leash. It felt like a hand steadying its back.

The sparrow, watching from the fence, said nothing at all.$q$, 'draft')
