-- Passage rg10-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8bbe8976-4d0e-56a2-b8c7-cd1ce5ac1fc1', 'rg10-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Version 1.0$q$, $q$Kofi Mensah spent most of the summer building an app that showed when city buses would reach the stop in front of Ridgeview High. He pulled the arrival times from the transit agency's public data, designed a simple screen with large numbers, and shared the link with his class in September. By October, four hundred students were using it.

On a rainy Wednesday, twelve of them missed the 7:15 bus. The app had shown it four minutes away when it was already pulling up to the curb. That night Kofi found the problem. When the transit agency switched to its winter schedule, his code had kept reading the old summer file. Every arrival time was off by a few minutes.

The fix took him twenty minutes. Deciding what to say took much longer. His friend Lena told him to update the app quietly. "Nobody reads update notes," she said. "If you announce a mistake, people will just remember the mistake."

Kofi typed an apology and deleted it three times. Finally he posted a short note at the top of the app. It explained what had gone wrong, which days had shown wrong times, and what he had changed. He added a button labeled Report a Problem and wrote that he would rather hear about errors from riders than discover them after someone missed a bus.

He expected complaints. Instead, by Friday he had nineteen messages. A few were annoyed, but most were reports: a stop name spelled wrong, a route that vanished on Sundays, a clock error on one student's older phone. A sophomore he had never met wrote that she had stopped trusting the app after Wednesday but had started again after reading the note.

Kofi fixed the stop name first. Then he opened a notebook and began a list titled Version 1.1.$q$, 'draft')
