   $q$The prefix de- can mean "remove," and hydr means "water." What does it mean that the meadow soil turned "dehydrated" after a drought?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The soil had soaked up extra water from the storm","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"The soil had been washed away by the moving river","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"The soil had lost most of its water and dried out","is_correct":true},{"text":"The soil had been packed down by heavy farm machines","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The soil had lost most of its water and dried out$q$, $q$De- plus hydr means "water removed," which matches soil that turned "hard" during a long drought. Soaking up water is the opposite; the passage says the storm "runs straight off the surface instead of soaking in."$q$, 'draft', '3a9ed744-5fee-5a76-b9b6-426a3d196390')
