-- Passage rg10-word-parts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a3d3f72a-582f-5852-b9e3-eadcd69bb2f6', 'rg10-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Last Five Hundred Meters$q$, $q$With five hundred meters left, Tomas Okonkwo-Reyes could hear nothing but the rhythm of eight oars striking the water together. His lungs burned. His respiration had become short and ragged, each breath pulled in on the forward slide and pushed out as the blade drove through the river.

Behind him sat Hana Mori, a sophomore who had joined the crew only that fall. She was an aspiring rower who had never raced in a final before, and at the start line her hands had been shaking on the oar handle. Now she matched the stroke perfectly.

In the stern, the coxswain, Dalia Brennan, faced the crew and called out the count. Her job was to steer and to keep eight tired people moving as one. "Ten strokes for the people who came to watch," she shouted. "Ten strokes for each other." Somehow the words were inspiring, even though Tomas could barely think. The boat lifted and surged.

Perspiration ran into his eyes, but he did not dare lift a hand to wipe it. Out of the corner of his vision he saw the bow of the rival boat, half a length ahead and then only a few seats ahead.

Then the horn sounded. The race was over, and so was the team's season. They had finished second by less than a second.

Later, on the dock, Hana admitted that she had nearly asked to sit out the final. "I watched all of you practice in the rain all spring," she said. "That was my inspiration." Tomas laughed, still out of breath, and realized the season had given them something more lasting than a medal.$q$, 'draft')
