   $q$The prefix in- means "into," and spir means "breathe," so to inspire once meant to breathe life into someone. Why were Dalia's words "inspiring"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They reminded the crew to breathe more slowly","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"They warned the crew that the rival was ahead","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"They filled the tired crew with fresh energy","is_correct":true},{"text":"They told the crew exactly how to steer the boat","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-related-idea"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They filled the tired crew with fresh energy$q$, $q$Something inspiring breathes new spirit into people, and right after her words "The boat lifted and surged." Steering is part of Dalia's job, but the words "for each other" motivate the crew rather than give directions.$q$, 'draft', 'a3d3f72a-582f-5852-b9e3-eadcd69bb2f6')
