  ('4f72d332-3b94-51f8-b069-b8a5e1a6b0b5', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null, 'medium',
   $q$The root jud means "judge," and the suffix -ious means "full of." What does Wen mean when he calls the new version "more judicious"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It takes a strong side against the district","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"It shows careful, fair judgment about the facts","is_correct":true},{"text":"It is written in the formal style of a court","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"It is longer and includes more quotations","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows careful, fair judgment about the facts$q$, $q$Judicious means "full of good judgment," and Wen praises the article because it did not "make the district look bad" or pretend the first number was right. Taking a strong side against the district is exactly what he says she avoided.$q$, 'draft', '5a67b95b-ebcb-5bb8-9b9d-986e52e1e701')
