insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1c3ac066-7dd6-5e4a-94b3-da82b7156735', 'rg10-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Farming the Mountainside$q$, $q$On steep mountain slopes, flat ground is rare and precious. For thousands of years, farmers in many parts of the world have solved this problem by carving hillsides into terraces, a series of level steps that climb the slope like a giant staircase. Each step holds a narrow field, and each field is braced by a wall of stone or packed earth.

Terraces do more than create flat space. Rain that would race down a bare slope, carrying soil with it, instead pauses on each level and soaks in. This greatly reduces erosion, the slow wearing away of land by water and wind. A well-built terrace system can keep the same soil in place for centuries.

Water management is the other half of the design. In dry regions, farmers built channels and small aqueducts to conduct water from mountain springs to the highest terraces. From there, the water trickled downward, field by field, so that a single source could irrigate an entire hillside.

Archaeologists studying abandoned terraces can learn a great deal from what remains. By examining the size of the walls and the layout of the channels, they can deduce how many people once farmed an area and how carefully their work was organized. Seeds and pollen trapped in the soil reveal which crops each terrace produced.

Some terraces have been farmed continuously across many generations, while others were left when families moved to cities. In several mountain regions, young farmers are now restoring old walls and channels. They argue that the ancient design suits a modern challenge: growing food on difficult land without washing the mountain away.$q$, 'draft')
