   $q$Which reason given by the railroad's supporters did Pollock's argument fail to address?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A canal boat could carry as much grain as forty wagons.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-counterclaims"},{"text":"Railroads would frighten livestock on nearby farms.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-counterclaims"},{"text":"Canal shipping cost about half as much as rail shipping.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-counterclaims"},{"text":"The canal would freeze for nearly four months every winter.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The canal would freeze for nearly four months every winter.$q$, $q$The winter freeze comes from the railroad supporters' pamphlet, and nothing in Pollock's case accounts for it. The cost figures, the forty wagons and the frightened livestock are all points Pollock himself made.$q$, 'draft', '4a94ef71-c422-5acd-9e2a-57dc871d959e')
