  ('0b28b183-654c-5c03-8eee-12addb517506', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null, 'medium',
   $q$How does the structure of the fourth paragraph ("Building something new is exciting... Keeping something working is quiet.") contribute to the speaker's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The matched pairs of sentences prove that new construction costs much more money than repair, which is the main point of the speech.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The matched pairs of sentences show that the speaker personally prefers quiet work to excitement, which explains the choice of bridge inspection as a career.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-purpose"},{"text":"The matched pairs of sentences list the steps of a repair project in order, which helps listeners follow how the bridge was fixed.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"},{"text":"The matched pairs of sentences contrast the attention new projects get with the neglect repair work gets, which sets up the warning about decay.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The matched pairs of sentences contrast the attention new projects get with the neglect repair work gets, which sets up the warning about decay.$q$, $q$The parallel sentences set "exciting" building beside "quiet" maintenance, and the "Yet" sentence then shows the danger of ignoring the quiet work. The paragraph makes no cost comparison; cost comes up later and only in general terms.$q$, 'draft', 'cb722d6f-3920-5e60-acc2-b1afa62fdcee')
