insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('81b91a97-1acd-5d90-9d86-ab6197b94f24', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Fifty-Two Years of Snow$q$, $q$From a speech by meteorologist Daniel Okafor at a dinner honoring volunteer weather observers.

Every morning at seven o'clock for fifty-two years, Eleanor Lindqvist walked to a white box in her backyard, read two thermometers, measured the snow or rain in a metal cylinder, and wrote the numbers in a notebook. She did this on her birthday. She did it the morning after her daughter's wedding. She did it during a blizzard, when she had to dig a path to the box with a kitchen pot because the shovel was buried.

She was never paid. Her observations went to the national weather service, which relies on many volunteers like her to fill the wide gaps between official stations.

I want to explain why her notebooks matter, because the answer is not obvious. A single day's temperature in one backyard tells us almost nothing. Neither does a single year. Weather is noisy; one winter is mild, the next is brutal, and no pattern can be seen up close. But fifty-two years of careful readings, taken at the same hour, in the same spot, with the same instruments, become something rare. They become a record steady enough to reveal slow changes that no single season could show, such as a first frost that arrives a little later each decade.

Satellites and automated sensors are wonderful tools, and they now collect more data in an hour than Eleanor gathered in her lifetime. But many of them have existed for only a fraction of the time her notebooks cover, and machines get replaced, upgraded, and moved. A long record is only as valuable as its consistency, and consistency is a human virtue before it is a technical one.

Our volunteer network is shrinking. Many of our longest-serving observers are in their eighties, and too few young people are taking their places. If you have ever wondered whether one person's daily habit could matter to science, look at Eleanor's notebooks. Then consider taking up the pencil.$q$, 'draft')
