-- Passage rg11-authors-purpose-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4b8fd98d-207e-59f4-8602-08897cb27068', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Case for Sitting Down$q$, $q$From remarks by urban planner Nadia Farouk at a neighborhood committee meeting on a downtown redesign.

I want to talk tonight about the least exciting item in the downtown plan: benches.

The current draft includes new bike lanes, a renovated fountain, and a lighting design that I think will be beautiful. It includes six benches along eleven blocks. I am here to ask for sixty.

That may sound like a strange thing to fight for. A bench is about the simplest object a city can buy. It does not appear in brochures. Nobody cuts a ribbon in front of one. But consider who actually needs a place to sit. An older man walking to the pharmacy who cannot manage eleven blocks without resting. A mother with a toddler who has decided, abruptly and without negotiation, that he will not walk another step. A teenager waiting for a bus that is twenty minutes late. A worker on a short break with nowhere to eat lunch but a curb.

For these people, a street without benches is not merely less comfortable. It is a street they will avoid. An older resident who cannot rest along the way may stop walking downtown altogether and will drive, or will stay home. When planners talk about making a district "walkable," we usually mean sidewalks and crosswalks. We rarely mean places to stop walking. Yet the two depend on each other.

There is also a simpler argument. Benches are where strangers become neighbors. People who sit near each other talk about the weather, the bus, the dog that just went by. These conversations are small, and they are also how a downtown stops being a place people pass through and becomes a place they belong.

The extra benches would cost less than one percent of the project budget. I would ask the committee to find that one percent. It may be the most generous thing in the entire plan.$q$, 'draft')
