  ('2ca5ef6c-f011-5bed-aecd-fa8e5ef18802', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null, 'medium',
   $q$How do the sentences "We rarely mean places to stop walking. Yet the two depend on each other." support the speaker's argument?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The apparent paradox admits that benches discourage walking, so the speaker proposes placing them only at the ends of blocks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-concession"},{"text":"The apparent paradox reframes benches as part of walkability, tying the request to a goal planners already value.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The apparent paradox shows that planners do not understand sidewalks, so the committee should hire new planners.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"The apparent paradox introduces a new topic about crosswalk safety, which the speaker discusses in the following paragraph.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-structure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The apparent paradox reframes benches as part of walkability, tying the request to a goal planners already value.$q$, $q$It seems odd that places to stop would help walking, but the speaker has just shown that people who cannot rest stop coming downtown, so benches serve the "walkable" goal. The next paragraph is about benches building community, not crosswalks.$q$, 'draft', '4b8fd98d-207e-59f4-8602-08897cb27068')
